lize there is levity in  this movie  and there are fun moments if you could  sort of mention that  yeah well jason siegel's really funny i  mean you put him in a movie and there  are going to be funny moments  and dakota is uh really fun and kind of  like a bright light and um she's gonna  be charming and you're gonna  it's gonna be hard to to uh dim that  light  um so that was really good casting by  gabriella  yeah i think dakota also had a lot of  the heavy lifting to do because she was  the person who had to be  playing someone who was dying and but  she also  by all accounts in real life her  character was you know real person  nicole and  that person as far as i anyone i talked  to  said that she was just the kind of  person that made everyone feel good  about being alive like one of those  people that just you like to be around  because they're fun  um and they're fun in a way that makes  everyone else have fun too  so she had to be that and also be  be playing the part of a mom who's who's  dying and is getting ready to say  goodbye to her kids  um so uh she had that responsibility of  making it sort of feel  almost like an uplifting movie and she  did a great job you've worked on a lot  of different genres a lot of different  projects  what is it like when you're playing the  very raw emotional scenes in the third  act  what was it like on set um were you guys  trying to keep levity on set were you  trying to maintain a certain emotional  thing you know what i mean yeah i mean  that doesn't always work for me i don't  like that sense of  oh here's a big day or everyone please  be quiet this is important hard work  i like to just come to work every day  sort of feeling the same and and do what  has to be done  and yeah i like it i don't know how many  movies i've made i've been  i've been really lucky i don't know 30  movies or something and  and so after a while you kind of get  used to it you know like you wait on  enough tables  you're kind of doing the same thing  every table comes up  you bring them take their order you go  to the kitchen you bring the food  um and sometimes there's a big order and  and you don't approach it any  differently you know you just stack  that a few extra plates on your arms and  you get it out there um  so those were some those are some hard  scenes you know just because uh  they are of their nature you know but  also there's cherry jones and there's  all these good actors around there just  doing stuff and i  you know just making it easy for  everyone by being good  so i kind of felt like i was in good  hands one of the things about this  project specifically is you filmed  in the real locations or you were in the  real place where  the story happened and you know you i'm  sure had people  family members possibly watching how did  that add to your  sort of how did that sort of affect you  on set did it affect you  knowing you had the the person you're  playing possibly right there  yeah well the person i was playing was  right there i mean matt teague who wrote  the article  and um he was the he was he was around  on set i talked to him you know going up  dinner with him  his kids were there um and his kids were  sort of the most important part  i just sort of thought like hey look  matt matt wrote this he created this  he wanted this to happen he's a grown-up  he understands  how movies can be different than real  life  um he he brought this on himself  and um but i for the kids i thought like  they probably didn't ask for this  you know and i i was i felt like it was  a great responsibility to make sure that  uh what i did whatever i could to  make the out the the final product be  something that was  did not um exacerbate or re-stimulate  the trauma of losing mom but in fact was  something that  um was another piece and sort of  the recovery and moving forward and and  and um  and you know there wasn't much i could  do but that was on my mind a lot  much more than like oh hey maybe this is  the neighbor who really  you knew these people um i felt like  that was  gabriela's uh territory the director she  has to worry about sort of like is this  movie something that  um is authentic enough that it uh  respects the real people and the real  story uh  you have been directing and i'm curious  how that  how directing has possibly changed the  way you are  on set have you noticed a different like  sort of way you conduct yourself or  anything because  you maybe have a better understanding of  what the director is going through  i might be more of a team player i mean  i might be you know  there was a time when i sort of felt  like what my job was  in some ways at odds with what the  director was doing i had to protect the  realism of the moment and a lot of  directors wanted that  for example i remember doing a scene in  this movie jesse james  and andrew dominic was a director and i  was sitting in a bathtub  and he's like you know i can't see you  from you know like  the angle that we're at this camera i  said well you're gonna  what do you wanna do stand up and take a  bath you gotta like you know  move the camera um he he and he was  really  uh he liked that approach you know is  that the actors have to sort of protect  the realism of what they would do  in this in a certain scene and he  worries about how to capture it  and then you know once i was a director  you know it could be a tough job and so  i was just you can't help but be more  empathetic after that  towards like okay we're running out of  time you know we're a team  we're all sort of doing this together  you really have to compromise sometimes  it's not always about  the actor sometimes it's about making  this  a shot that the camera team can actually  pull off  without having to take 30 minutes to  rebuild a room or something  um so i think i hope it's made me a  little bit more of a collaborator  in that way and um sometimes on the set  of this movie i saw gabrielle and i  thought